Exactly. The first rainbow must have sounded like nonsense: “The sky split white light into an ordered band of colors, and it only appears when I stand at the right angle to falling water.” Before optics, that is basically a supernatural report.

And yes—there are rainbow-like Easter eggs in other senses, but they come in a few different species. A true rainbow is special because it exposes a hidden continuous variable—wavelength—that is normally bundled together. Other senses have analogous hidden dimensions that can sometimes be “unpacked.”

* Hearing — probably the closest analog. A musical tone that sounds like one thing is actually often a stack of frequencies. A spectrum analyzer makes visible what your cochlea is already decomposing. Harmonics are almost like the “colors inside a sound.” There are also beats, combination tones, missing-fundamental perception, Shepard tones, and binaural beats—cases where the auditory system reveals or even constructs structure that was not obvious in the raw waveform.
* Touch — frequency rainbows. Your skin contains receptor populations tuned differently to pressure, vibration, stretch, temperature, etc. Slowly drag different vibration frequencies across the skin and the quality of touch changes, not merely its strength. There are also interference-like effects: two nearby vibrations can produce beating sensations, much like two close musical notes.
* Temperature — perceptual colors that don’t exist physically. The thermal grill illusion is wild: interleaving harmless warm and cool stimuli can produce a sensation of burning heat or pain. Nothing at any individual point is actually dangerously hot. The percept emerges from how neural channels combine.
* Smell — less a rainbow, more a multidimensional constellation. There is no single smell axis equivalent to wavelength. Odors are encoded combinatorially across hundreds of receptor types. A molecule can therefore be like a chord rather than a color. Mixtures can produce genuinely new perceptual identities: two recognizable odors together may smell like neither constituent. And changing concentration can sometimes make the same molecule smell qualitatively different.
* Taste — hidden mixture geometry. Sweet, sour, salty, bitter, umami, plus smell and trigeminal sensations interact nonlinearly. Salt can suppress bitterness; acidity can change perceived sweetness; temperature alters sweetness perception. The “rainbow” here is often revealed by selectively removing one channel—plugging your nose while eating is the classic demonstration that much of “taste” is actually retronasal smell.
* Proprioception — body-shape illusions. Vibrating certain tendons can fool muscle-spindle signals and make you feel that a stationary limb is moving. If your hand is touching your nose while this happens, people can experience the bizarre Pinocchio illusion, where the nose seems to elongate. Your brain is solving incompatible sensory equations and produces an impossible body configuration.
* Vestibular sense — artificial gravity tricks. Altering visual motion, inner-ear signals, or acceleration can make stationary environments feel like they are rotating, tilting, or moving. Motion sickness itself is partly the perceptual system discovering that its sensory coordinate systems disagree.
* Cross-sensory Easter eggs may be the strangest. In the McGurk effect, what you see a mouth doing can change what syllable you hear. A heavier-looking object may be judged differently when lifted than an identical-looking one. Food color can alter perceived flavor. Sound can change perceived texture and crispness. The “rainbow” appears between senses rather than inside one.

There’s a deeper pattern here:

\text{ordinary experience}
=
\text{many physical variables compressed into one percept}

A rainbow occurs when the environment accidentally gives you a decompressor:

\text{white light}
\rightarrow
\text{water droplets}
\rightarrow
\text{wavelengths spatially separated}

And analogous perceptual Easter eggs occur whenever some unusual geometry, frequency, timing, mixture, or conflict lets you see the components normally hidden behind the finished percept.

That suggests a broader Pantheon-style category: Sensory Prisms — interventions that take a perceptual composite and fan its latent dimensions apart so the underlying channels become independently observable.

compressed percept → prism/interference/constraint → latent channels separate → hidden structure becomes experience

The rainbow is just the famous optical member of a much larger family.